    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 242057\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n157 PM MST Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The chances for showers and thunderstorms will continue through\n  tonight, with the highest potential mostly focused over the\n  foothills and higher terrain areas north and east of Phoenix.\n\n- After drying out temporarily this weekend, rain chances will\n  return to the region late Sunday into early next week as \n  tropical moisture gets pulled northward into the Desert \n  Southwest.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures will persist through\n  the weekend before noticeably cooling early next week.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Friday/... \nAn extensive area of showers and storms was ongoing through much \nof the morning hours, especially to the south and west of the \nPhoenix Metro Area across much of western Maricopa county \nextending into portions of La Paz and Yuma Counties. All of this \nactivity was associated with a mid-level low currently spinning \nover southern AZ. This activity should gradually weaken heading \ninto afternoon hours. The mid-level low is expected to move northward\ninto central AZ by this evening. However, despite there being \ngood ascent, the extensive cloud cover and rainfall activity from \nearlier has overworked the atmosphere to the point that it is \nlikely that little to no activity, with the exception of portions \nof Gila County, will materialize through the rest of this \nafternoon through early this evening as depicted by the latest \nruns of the hi-res guidance. By late this evening into the \novernight period, however, as the mid-level low migrates into \ncentral AZ, the orographic and synoptic lift will combine to lead \nto an area of showers and storms forming mostly across the \nfoothills and higher terrain areas to the north and east of \nPhoenix with only isolated coverage across the lower deserts. As \nthe mid-level low lifts to the northeast on Friday, westerly flow \nwill encroach much of the region and advect much drier air, \nessentially ending rain/storm chances. \n\nGiven the extensive cloud cover still in place, afternoon high \ntemperatures across the south-central AZ lower deserts, in \nparticular, are likely to struggle to reach 90 degrees with many \nlocations staying in the 80s. Across the western deserts, lesser \ncloud cover with more sunshine will cause readings to top out in \nthe mid to upper 90s. Mostly clear skies on Friday should allow \ntemperatures across south-central AZ to rebound to more seasonal \nlevels, in the mid to upper 90s, with slightly warmer readings\ninto the lower 100s across the western deserts. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Saturday through Wednesday/... \nDry westerly flow is expected to persist through at least \nSaturday with large scale troughing remaining over the Pac NW \nwhile the subtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east \nTX into the interior of MX. Due to the presence of the ridge to \nour east, 500 mb hghts are expected to peak around 586-588 dam \nSaturday afternoon. This will result in highs topping out between \n100-105 degrees across the lower deserts (4-6 degrees above \nnormal) under mostly sunny skies and zero chance of rain. From \nSunday into early next week, there does look to be a significant \ndegree of cooling as tropical moisture arrives and rain chances \nincrease. Highs will likely fall below 100 degrees across the \nlower deserts on Sunday and continue to cool to the upper 80s and \nlower 90s Monday through Wednesday.\n\nOur attention then turns to the tropical Pacific Sunday into early \nnext week where potential moisture from two weakening hurricanes \n(Polo and Odalys) will come into play. There is still a high degree \nof uncertainty as to a) how far north the residual moisture will be \nshed out ahead of these decaying storms, and b) where the plume(s) \nof moisture will set up. Another major question mark will be just \nhow deep and progressive a developing trough of low pressure will be \nover the Intermountain West. All of these factors will have to line \nup perfectly for our forecast area to get significant rainfall. \nBoth the GEFS and EPS are telling a different story with varying \ndegrees of moisture and timing and intensity of said trough which \nis really complicating things. The latest NHC track for Hurricane\nPolo shows the storm making landfall along the coast of the Baja \nCalifornia by Monday which if aligned with the passage of the \naforementioned trough would draw a large plume of moisture into \ninterior MX and and much of AZ. NBM PoPs have already increased to\naround 20-30% with an initial band of moisture on Sunday, but \nwith the main lift arriving on Monday, PoPs are now widespread \n30-60% across our forecast area. The remnant tropical moisture may\nlinger into Tuesday before fully exiting the region on Wednesday.\nIt is also worth pointing out that some ensemble members are \nshowing significant rainfall totals in excess of 1-2\" across \nsouthcentral AZ Monday-Tuesday but there is still significant \nspread between members. Therefore, we will likely have to wait \nuntil this weekend for things to become more clear. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1755Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main aviation concern will be the potential for further\nSHRA/VCTS activity this afternoon and tonight. However, forecast\nconfidence is low regarding coverage an impacts as these next\nrounds will be highly conditional on how the rest of the day\nunfolds. With that being said, there is somewhat better confidence\nthat timing of possible overnight convection would fall sometime\nbetween 08-13Z, but even then, rain chances are only 20-30%. Due\nto the uncertainty, frequent adjustments/amendments may take \nplace over the next several hours. Outside of convective periods, \nwinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with speeds generally \naob 12 kt. SCT-BKN skies will be common through tonight, with the \nlowest CIGs remaining aob 6 kft. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nOutside of a brief window of breeziness at KIPL this afternoon,\nwinds will be light and favor familiar diurnal directional trends,\nthough extended periods of VRB conditions will be common during\nthe forecast window. SCT to sometimes BKN skies will be present\nover the region until clearing takes by Friday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWidely scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible\nthrough tonight, mainly focused over the higher terrain of the \neastern districts. Localized gusty and erratic winds will be \npossible in the vicinity of any storm that develops. Rain chances \nwill decrease Friday into this weekend before much better chances \nfor wetting rainfall arrives early next week. MinRHs will range \nfrom 25-60% today with a gradual decrease to around 15-30% \nregion-wide Saturday. Significant moisture return is expected \nacross the eastern districts by Monday which will keep RH values \nabove 40% with excellent overnight recoveries. Winds will \ngenerally follow diurnal patterns with speeds around 10-15 mph \nwith typical afternoon gusts in the upper teens.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno/Lojero\n",
